# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Work with multiple observables {#work-with-multiple-observables}"
msgstr "Magtrabaho gamit ang maraming observable {#work-with-multiple-observables}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `qiskit_addon_obp.backpropagate` method allows one to pass in a sequence of observables. This simplifies the workflow when dealing with multiple target observables.\n"
msgstr "Ang method na `qiskit_addon_obp.backpropagate` ay nagpapahintulot na magpasa ng sequence ng mga observable. Pinapasimple nito ang workflow kapag maraming target na observable.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We explicitly mention this here to teach you how the truncation strategy handles such a case. For the sake of this example, we add an extra observable to the one which we have been using so far:\n"
msgstr "Tahasan naming binabanggit ito rito para ituro kung paano hinahawakan ng truncation strategy ang ganitong kaso. Para sa halimbawang ito, magdadagdag tayo ng isa pang observable sa ginagamit natin hanggang ngayon:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, we repeat the first experiment from this tutorial., but with two observables to backpropagate the circuit into.\n"
msgstr "Panghuli, uulitin natin ang unang eksperimento mula sa tutorial na ito, pero may dalawang observable na paglalagyan ng backpropagation ng circuit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For this example, this does not affect the number of slices that could be backpropagated. However, we can see that the two observables resulted in different numbers of Pauli terms and commuting groups.\n"
msgstr "Sa halimbawang ito, hindi nito naaapektuhan ang bilang ng mga slice na na-backpropagate. Gayunpaman, makikita natin na ang dalawang observable ay nagresulta sa magkaibang bilang ng mga Pauli term at commuting group.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The plots below help illustrate how the backpropagation algorithm handles multiple observables.\n"
msgstr "Tinutulungan ng mga plot sa ibaba na ilarawan kung paano hinahawakan ng backpropagation algorithm ang maraming observable.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, the plots in the **top left**, **top right**, and **bottom left** show that the budget for truncating terms is set individually for each observable. In other words, both observables have the ability to truncate terms assuming an error of `0.001` per backpropagated slice.\n"
"Due to the different nature of the observables, this results in different consumption of the budget. In this example, they exhibit a lot of overlap, which does not always happen.\n"
msgstr ""
"Una, ipinapakita ng mga plot sa **kaliwang itaas**, **kanang itaas**, at **kaliwang ibaba** na ang budget para sa pag-truncate ng mga term ay itinatakda nang hiwalay para sa bawat observable. Sa madaling salita, parehong may kakayahan ang dalawang observable na mag-truncate ng mga term sa pag-aakalang may error na `0.001` bawat na-backpropagate na slice.\n"
"Dahil sa magkaibang katangian ng mga observable, nagreresulta ito sa magkaibang paggamit ng budget. Sa halimbawang ito, malaki ang overlap nila, na hindi laging nangyayari.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The **bottom right** plot illustrates that `max_qwc_groups` takes _all_ observables into account. That means that the terms of all observables are grouped together to arrive at one final number of qubit-wise commuting groups, which is compared against `max_qwc_paulis`. The same is done for the `max_paulis` threshold (not discussed in this notebook), which allows you to set a limit on the number of Pauli terms across all observables.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapakita ng plot sa **kanang ibaba** na isinasaalang-alang ng `max_qwc_groups` ang _lahat_ ng observable. Ibig sabihin, pinagsasama-sama ang mga term ng lahat ng observable para makarating sa iisang huling bilang ng qubit-wise commuting group, na ikinukumpara sa `max_qwc_paulis`. Ganito rin ang ginagawa para sa threshold na `max_paulis` (hindi tinalakay sa notebook na ito), na nagpapahintulot na magtakda ng limitasyon sa bilang ng mga Pauli term sa lahat ng observable.\n"

